# Points, Lines, and Angles: Geometry's Atoms

> Every diagram in geometry — however complicated — is built from three
> primitives: points, lines, and angles. Learn their rules first.

## 1. The Undefined Starting Points

[definition]
A **point** names a location; it has no size. A **line** is a straight
path extending forever in both directions; it has length but no width.
A **ray** starts at a point and extends forever one way; a **segment**
joins two endpoints and has a measurable length.
[/definition]

Through any two distinct points passes **exactly one** line — geometry's
first great "obviously true, endlessly useful" fact.

## 2. Angles

An **angle** measures the turn between two rays sharing an endpoint
(the vertex), usually in degrees:

| Type | Measure |
| --- | --- |
| Acute | between 0° and 90° |
| Right | exactly 90° |
| Obtuse | between 90° and 180° |
| Straight | exactly 180° |
| Reflex | between 180° and 360° |

## 3. Angle Facts That Power Everything

- Angles on a straight line sum to **180°**.
- Angles around a point sum to **360°**.
- **Vertically opposite** angles (formed by two crossing lines) are equal.
- When lines are parallel:

@diagram tree id="parallel-angles" layout="tree" aria="Angle pairs made by parallel lines"
  "Parallel lines + transversal" -> "Corresponding angles: equal"
  "Parallel lines + transversal" -> "Alternate angles: equal"
  "Parallel lines + transversal" -> "Co-interior angles: sum to 180"
@/diagram

## Example

Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle is 70°. Find the rest:

- Corresponding angle: **70°**
- Alternate angle: **70°**
- Co-interior partner: $180° - 70° = \mathbf{110°}$
- Vertically opposite pairs repeat the pattern on the other side.

## 4. Triangles Before You Even Draw One

The **angle sum of a triangle is always 180°** — a consequence of the
parallel-line facts above (draw a line through one vertex parallel to
the opposite side and watch the angles transfer).

So in a triangle with angles $a$, $b$, $c$:

@math label="eq:triangle-sum"
a + b + c = 180^{\circ}
@/math

An **exterior angle** equals the sum of the two remote interior angles.

[important title="Why proofs matter"]
Each fact here follows from earlier ones — parallel-line facts give the
triangle angle sum, which later gives polygon sums
$(n-2) \times 180°$. Geometry is a chain of small certainties, not a
pile of formulas to memorise.
[/important]

## Key Takeaways

- Point, line, ray, segment are the atoms everything else is built from.
- Straight-line 180° and around-point 360° anchor all angle chasing.
- Parallel lines create equal alternate/corresponding pairs and
  co-interior pairs that sum to 180°.
